} else {
    Q.kpo_public_credit -= 3;
    if (Q.government_party == "ko") {
        Q.ko_relation += 4;
        Q.ko_coalition_dissent -= 3;
    } else if (Q.government_party == "psl") {
        Q.psl_relation += 4;
        Q.psl_coalition_dissent -= 3;
    } else if (Q.government_party == "p2050") {
        Q.p2050_relation += 4;
        Q.p2050_coalition_dissent -= 3;
    } else {
        Q.government_coalition_dissent -= 3;
    }
    Q.barons_dissent += 2;
    Q.progressives_dissent += 3;
    Q.kpo_last_credit_change -= 3;
    Q.kpo_last_summary =
        "Flagship projects move, while coalition credit centralises around " +
        Q.prime_minister + ".";
}
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 4;
Q.news_headline =
    Q.prime_minister + " concentrates the first recovery projects";
!}

The money goes into a short list of large schemes that can be opened by a
minister with a ribbon and a camera. They are real projects and they will be
finished; nobody chose them for their ability to reach the places with the
worst bus timetables.

[? if government_party = "lewica": Our own Chancellery took the decision, which
spares us a coalition argument and hands us the whole of the criticism.?][? if
government_party != "lewica": **[+ prime_minister +]** takes the podium.
Lewica's ministers are in the second row of the photograph, having supplied the
votes that made the fund available.?]

- @kpo_result: Concentrate the tranche

@payment_cofinance
title: Hold a co-financing reserve for the year-end budget
subtitle: Holds one released tranche against December's co-financing; nothing visible this year.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and kpo_first_payment_paid = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.kpo_funds_spent += 1;
Q.budget += 2;
Q.kpo_delivery += 1;
Q.kpo_public_credit += 1;
Q.fiscal_stress = Math.max(0, Q.fiscal_stress - 2);
Q.ko_relation += 2;
Q.kpo_last_credit_change += 1;
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 1;
Q.kpo_last_summary = "A reserve creates budget capacity, though few voters can see it yet.";
Q.news_headline = "KPO co-financing gives the coalition room before the budget";
!}

The tranche is not spent. It is parked against the co-financing the December
budget will need, where it will quietly prevent a fight about the deficit that
would otherwise cost us a social line.

It is the most useful thing available and the least visible. No ribbon, no
site, no announcement — just a number in the Finance Ministry that makes next
winter survivable. Our organisers ask, reasonably, what they are supposed to
say at the door.

- @kpo_result: Hold the reserve

@payment_correction
title: Concede the defects and publish a sixty-day correction file
subtitle: Admit the defects, repair the file and make KO share the embarrassment.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and kpo_first_payment_paid = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.kpo_milestones += 4;
Q.kpo_delivery += 2;
Q.kpo_deadline_pressure = Math.max(0, Q.kpo_deadline_pressure - 4);
Q.kpo_public_credit += 1;
Q.government_procedural_restraint += 3;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.kpo_last_credit_change = 1;
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 2;
Q.kpo_last_summary = "Owning the delay limits the panic and creates a credible correction route.";
Q.news_headline = "The government publishes the defects in its own KPO file";
!}

We put the defects in writing before a Commission letter forces us to: which
annexes were weak, who signed them off, and the sixty days in which each will
be repaired. It is an unpleasant document to publish under your own government.

The unpleasantness is the value. A file that admits its faults on schedule is a
file Brussels can keep working with, and KO — which drafted several of the weak
annexes — cannot now let us carry the admission alone.

- @kpo_result: Publish the correction file

@payment_opposition_dashboard
title: Track contracts, milestones and beneficiaries outside government
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource for a public contracts dashboard.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The opposition cannot maintain the contracts database.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.kpo_public_credit += 3;
Q.media_capacity += 3;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.local_network += 2;
Q.kpo_last_credit_change += 3;
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 0;
Q.kpo_last_summary = "The Left cannot allocate the transfer, but it can make ownership harder to fake.";
Q.news_headline = "An opposition KPO dashboard follows every contract";
!}

Contracts, beneficiaries and milestone dates go onto a public page that
updates whenever the government publishes anything. It is unglamorous work: two
staff, a spreadsheet and a great deal of patience with procurement portals.

Journalists start using it because nothing else assembles the same information
in one place. We cannot direct a złoty of this money. We can make it
significantly harder for anyone to claim a project that has not started.

- @kpo_result: Open the dashboard

@payment_opposition_record
title: Request the public contracts register and record unanswered questions
subtitle: Always available in opposition. Uses existing disclosures; no permanent dashboard or implementation credit.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.kpo_scrutiny += 1;
Q.kpo_public_credit += 1;
Q.kpo_last_credit_change += 1;
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 0;
Q.kpo_last_summary = "A basic parliamentary record exposes unanswered contract questions without pretending to monitor every beneficiary.";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica files a basic public record of the first KPO contracts";
!}

We request the contracts register through the ordinary disclosure route and
log every question the ministry declines to answer. There is no dashboard, no
staff and no launch — only a numbered paper trail.

It is the version of scrutiny available to a party that cannot fund a
monitoring team. Thin, but a thin record still contradicts a confident minister
eighteen months from now.

- @kpo_result: File the register request


