Q.news_headline = "The Left supplies votes for a narrow KPO correction";
!}

We vote for the narrow corrective act because labour projects and municipal
works are in it, and say plainly that we are voting for those and not for the
government's stewardship.

The act passes. Implementation stays entirely with a cabinet we do not sit in,
which means we have taken responsibility for the vote and none for the result —
a position that is honest, useful, and impossible to put on a poster.

- @kpo_result: Supply the votes

@midterm_opposition_audit
title: Refuse a rescue until contracts and missed milestones are published
subtitle: Demand the contracts first and accept that the payment clock keeps running.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.kpo_public_credit += 4;
Q.media_capacity += 3;
Q.ko_relation -= 5;
Q.psl_relation -= 2;
Q.kpo_deadline_pressure += 2;
Q.public_trust += 1;
Q.kpo_last_credit_change = 4;
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 0;
Q.kpo_last_summary = "The audit line is politically clear and leaves the payment clock running.";
Q.news_headline = "The Left demands the missing KPO contracts before a rescue vote";
!}

No rescue votes until the contracts and the missed milestones are published.
The demand is clean, defensible and easy to explain at any doorstep in the
country.

The payment clock does not pause while we make it. Mayors with half-built
projects understand the principle and would still rather have the money, and
some of them say so on regional television.

- @kpo_result: Demand the contracts


@kpo_budget_gate_2025
